{
  "rule": "gradient_mpas",
  "metric": "Linf",
  "expected_min_order": 1.7,
  "notes": "MPAS edge-normal gradient (grad phi)_e = (phi[c2]-phi[c1])/dc_edge[e], c1=cells_on_edge[1,e], c2=cells_on_edge[2,e]. MMS: linear cartesian field phi=V*r, V=(1,0.37,-0.6); analytic edge-normal surface gradient grad_s(phi).n_e = V.n_e. Unlike the flux-form divergence / nn_diffusion operators (first-order in L-inf on the irregular dual), the edge-normal gradient is SECOND order in L-inf: for a field linear in cartesian coordinates the chord difference phi[c2]-phi[c1] is exact and dc_edge is the exact cell-center distance, so the per-edge truncation is O(h^2) uniformly (the chord-direction vs edge-normal-direction mismatch is O(h^2)). Observed L-inf orders over the x1.642 -> x1.2562 -> x1.10242 unit-sphere ladder are approximately [1.99, 2.00]; we require min order >= 1.7. See Ringler et al. (2010, J. Comput. Phys. 229:3065-3090, Eq. 22) for the TRiSK gradient."
}
